\title{Quantum computation with realistic magic state factories}
\author{Joe O'Gorman\thanks{Department of Materials, University of Oxford, Oxford, OX1 3PH, United Kingdom.} \and Earl T. Campbell\thanks{Department of Physics \& Astronomy, University of Sheffield, Sheffield, S3 7RH, United Kingdom.}}
\date{arXiv:1605.07197v2 [quant-ph] 24 Dec 2016}

\begin{abstract}
Leading approaches to fault-tolerant quantum computation dedicate a significant portion of the hardware to computational factories that churn out high-fidelity ancillas called magic states. Consequently, efficient and realistic factory design is of paramount importance. Here we present the most detailed resource assessment to date of magic state factories within a surface code quantum computer, along the way introducing a number of new techniques. We show that the block codes of Bravyi and Haah [Phys.~Rev.~A \textbf{86}, 052329 (2012)] have been systematically undervalued; we track correlated errors both numerically and analytically, providing fidelity estimates without appeal to the union bound. We also introduce a subsystem code realisation of these protocols with constant time and low ancilla cost. Additionally, we confirm that magic state factories have space-time costs that scale as a constant factor of surface code costs. We find that the magic state factory required for post-classical factoring can be as small as \(6.3\times 10^{6}\) data qubits, ignoring ancilla qubits, assuming \(10^{-4}\) error gates, and the availability of long range interactions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Key formulas}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{15-to-1 distillation (Bravyi-Kitaev):}  \(p_{\text{out}} \approx 35\, p_{\text{in}}^{3}\).
  \item \textbf{Surface-code logical error rate:}  \(P_L(d,\,p_g) = d \cdot (100\,p_g)^{(d+1)/2}\).
  \item \textbf{Raw magic-state input error:}  \(\epsilon_{\text{in}} = 0.4\,p_g\)  (Li injection, Ref.~[42]).
  \item \textbf{Per-magic-state target:}  \(\epsilon_{\text{target}} = 1 - (P_{\text{succ,alg}})^{1/N}\).
\end{itemize}

